# templates/launchd/OWNED-BY-OTHER-INSTALLERS — launchd labels THIS REPO installs (or adopts) from a
# surface other than `scripts/install-launchd.sh`'s template set (#1867 review P1).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# `install-launchd.sh --status` reports any installed `com.eldato.*` / `com.tortoise.*` plist with no
# template as UNMANAGED and exits non-zero. That instrument is right, but its declared set is ONE
# directory, and this repo installs launchd jobs from other surfaces: the harness controller
# `com.eldato.orchestrator-loop`, rendered by `scripts/orchestrator-harness/install.sh` from
# `scripts/orchestrator-harness/launchd.plist.template` and cycled through `launchd-cycle.sh` — it is
# deliberately NOT in this directory, because that installer owns its own lifecycle.
#
# Flagging it made `--status` exit 1 forever on the reference Mac layout, and the caller that gates on
# it hard-FAILs: `scripts/fleet-schedule.py` → `FAIL … SCHEDULE NOT VERIFIED` →
# `activate-harness.sh` → `fleet-schedule … FAILING`. A drift instrument whose healthy state is red
# is one an operator learns to ignore — or silences by deleting a plist the controller needs.
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE
# -----------------
# A label that (a) matches the UNMANAGED scope, (b) is installed — or is adopted and supervised — by
# another surface of THIS repo, named in the entry's own comment with its file, and (c) therefore has
# no template here. A job that should be GONE belongs in `RETIRED` instead (that is a removal). A job
# nobody declares is exactly what UNMANAGED is for — do not add it here to quiet the alarm.
#
# ⛔ WHY THE REMEDY MAKES THIS LIST LOAD-BEARING, NOT COSMETIC: `--status` prints, for every label it
# reports, `launchctl bootout … && rm -f …`. A missing entry therefore does not merely print a false
# alarm — it hands the operator the command that DELETES a live job belonging to the box's own
# controller. Both entries below were found that way (#1867 review P1, rounds 4-5).
#
# ⛔ DRIFT-GATED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS: `scripts/install-launchd.test.sh` §18 asserts (1) every entry
# below still has a DECLARING SURFACE in this repo — matched against `LABEL="…"`, `BASE_LABEL="…"`,
# `LABEL="${LABEL:-com.…}"`, an inline `<string>com.…</string>` plist label, or a literal
# `Library/LaunchAgents/com.….plist` path, so an entry cannot go stale — and (2) the REVERSE: every
# `com.eldato.*` / `com.tortoise.*` label any installer in this repo writes into `LaunchAgents` is a
# template, RETIRED, or listed here, so a MISSING entry fails the suite instead of becoming a delete
# advice. A gate that can only drift one way cannot notice the direction that does harm.
#
#   com.eldato.orchestrator-loop   ← scripts/orchestrator-harness/install.sh (BASE_LABEL + LABEL)
#                                   also scripts/orchestrator-bringup.sh (LABEL)
#   com.eldato.lane-arm            ← scripts/install-lane-arm-timer.sh (inline plist label;
#                                   the label is NOT a `LABEL="com.…"` assignment there — its
#                                   `LABEL=eldato-lane-arm` is the systemd unit name — which is why
#                                   the drift gate reads the inline plist spelling too)
#   com.eldato.orchestrator-stack  ← ADOPTED, not installed: it is the supervision job a box already
#                                   carries (`scripts/orchestrator-bringup.sh`, "here the loaded job is
#                                   com.eldato.orchestrator-stack -> /bin/bash $O/supervisor.sh, while
#                                   install.sh renders com.eldato.orchestrator-loop") and the DEFAULT
#                                   target of `scripts/orchestrator-harness/watchdog-loop.sh`
#                                   (`LABEL="${LABEL:-com.eldato.orchestrator-stack}"`). No installer
#                                   in this repo writes its plist — which is precisely why flagging it
#                                   aimed a `bootout && rm -f` at the box's live controller.
com.eldato.orchestrator-loop
com.eldato.lane-arm
com.eldato.orchestrator-stack
